1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,900
The body hadn't even grown cold yet.

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:06,660
Alice Monaghan smelled it before the door
was fully open,

3
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:10,420
not the smell she had learned to expect in
hotel rooms,

4
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:15,500
the warm, closed smell of sleep and
laundered fabric,

5
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:21,180
and whatever a human being exhales across
seven or eight hours in a sealed space.

6
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:25,700
This was the other thing, the outdoor
thing,

7
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:31,900
the smell that arrives after a summer storm
when you step outside and the rain has

8
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:34,920
moved on and the air has been rearranged
by it.

9
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:41,540
Sharp, clean, not comfortable, a clarity
with no warmth in it.

10
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:46,800
Alice Monaghan had grown up in County Cork
before the Atlantic crossing.

11
00:00:47,620 --> 00:00:50,040
She knew what summer lightning left
behind.

12
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:53,460
It was January, the windows were closed.

13
00:00:53,460 --> 00:00:57,480
She could see the curtains, heavy and
still,

14
00:00:58,340 --> 00:01:00,780
against the cold light of Manhattan
morning.

15
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:03,800
The pass key was in her hand.

16
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:07,160
Room 3, 3, 2, 7.

17
00:01:08,780 --> 00:01:11,300
Hotel New Yorker, 33rd floor.

18
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:17,520
The Do Not Disturb sign had been on the
handle since approximately January 5th,

19
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:22,440
three days, and the guest management record
noted that the occupant had not requested

20
00:01:22,440 --> 00:01:25,560
food, laundry, or any contact whatsoever.

21
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:28,260
This was known to be a preference.

22
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:32,400
Long standing. Staff had been told.

23
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:35,680
But three days was three days.

24
00:01:36,980 --> 00:01:38,660
The door swung inward.

25
00:01:39,760 --> 00:01:42,060
The smell did not wait for her to step
into it.

26
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:48,240
It stepped into her. The way concentrated air
moves when it has been sealed with something,

27
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:50,980
and finally finds an opening.

28
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,260
Outward, into the corridor, filling the
space around her face before she had crossed

29
00:01:57,260 --> 00:01:59,460
the threshold. Sharp.

30
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:03,160
The taste of it was at the back of her
palette now.

31
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:08,740
That metallic quality, like a copper penny
held too long in a closed hand.

32
00:02:09,620 --> 00:02:13,900
She stepped in. The room was colder than
the corridor.

33
00:02:14,620 --> 00:02:16,960
Not because any window had been left open.

34
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:19,640
The curtains hung undisturbed.

35
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:22,160
The frame sealed against January.

36
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:25,280
The cold was of a different kind.

37
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:30,160
The cold of a room that has been without
the movement of a living body for

38
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:31,560
longer than a room should be.

39
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:34,160
The radiator was ticking.

40
00:02:35,380 --> 00:02:40,960
The air itself had simply withdrawn, gone
thin in the way the air goes thin in

41
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:45,800
closed spaces. When the thing that
displaced it is no longer displacing it.

42
00:02:46,580 --> 00:02:49,260
On the windowsill. Three feathers.

43
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:56,340
Gray tipped. They lay against each other at
angles that did not suggest they had drifted

44
00:02:56,340 --> 00:03:00,200
there. Did not suggest the casual landing
of shed feathers.

45
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:02,880
They lay as if placed.

46
00:03:03,720 --> 00:03:07,960
On the desk, and on the bedside table, and
on the floor beside the bed.

47
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:11,640
Hotel napkins. Dozens of them.

48
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:17,860
Equations. A handwriting that did not slow
for the printed borders of the paper.

49
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:20,360
That crossed the edges without adjusting.

50
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:23,020
That treated the napkin's edge as a
boundary.

51
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:26,620
The mind that produced these marks had
simply declined to recognize.

52
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:29,960
In the bed. The body.

53
00:03:31,580 --> 00:03:34,600
Arranged with a precision that did not
suggest rest.

54
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:40,800
The sheets pulled to the specific tightness
of someone who had understood this would

55
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:43,980
be the last arrangement. And had made it
accordingly.

56
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:51,500
Alice Monahan's professional composure broke
the way composure breaks when the body outpaces

57
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:53,860
the mind. Not dramatically.

58
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:57,300
Not with sound. In a breath.

59
00:03:58,520 --> 00:04:01,700
The inhalation she took when she saw the
figure in the bed.

60
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:04,900
And the specific quality of the air she
pulled in.

61
00:04:06,020 --> 00:04:09,160
Lightning. The air tasted of lightning.

62
00:04:10,380 --> 00:04:14,020
She backed out. She closed the door.

63
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:16,920
She went to find her supervisor.

64
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:19,240
And she did not say anything about the
smell.

65
00:04:20,220 --> 00:04:22,820
Not to the supervisor. Not to anyone
officially.

66
00:04:24,060 --> 00:04:25,720
Later, to her sister.

67
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:31,340
She would say it once. The room smelled
like the air before a thunderstorm.

68
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:33,260
She would say it.

69
00:04:33,860 --> 00:04:37,860
And then uncertain whether the description
made any kind of sense.

70
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:40,620
She would not say it again.

71
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:44,540
What was in that room? Not the body.

72
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:48,240
The body is the easy answer and the wrong
question.

73
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:52,580
What was in that room on the morning of
January 8th,

74
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:57,460
1943, that had been in it during the three
days the sign was on the door.

75
00:04:58,170 --> 00:05:01,800
That had left behind the smell of a storm
that did not exist.

76
00:05:02,680 --> 00:05:05,420
That is where this investigation begins.

77
00:05:06,620 --> 00:05:08,540
That is what we are going to find.

78
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:12,100
July 10th, 1856.

79
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:18,900
The village of Smiljan. In the Lika region
of the military frontier of the Austrian

80
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:21,740
Empire. High plateau country.

81
00:05:22,380 --> 00:05:23,840
Rocky limestone cast.

82
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:30,380
Cold even in summer. The kind of landscape
that does not encourage softness in the people

83
00:05:30,380 --> 00:05:33,880
who live on it. Two o 'clock in the
morning.

84
00:05:35,500 --> 00:05:36,920
A violent lightning storm.

85
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:43,580
The midwife crossed the threshold of the
Tesla farmhouse and the hair on her arms stood

86
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:45,880
up. Not metaphorically.

87
00:05:46,660 --> 00:05:48,400
The fine hair on the forearms.

88
00:05:49,160 --> 00:05:50,720
Straight up. Rigid.

89
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:56,080
Reaching towards the ceiling the way hair
reaches towards a charged surface before contact.

90
00:05:57,180 --> 00:06:02,320
She felt it as a sudden wrongness in her
own skin and then she felt the

91
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:07,240
first spark. It jumped from the hem of her
skirt to the floorboard with a crack

92
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:12,520
she felt in her teeth. Not the thin blue
snap of dry winter static.

93
00:06:13,340 --> 00:06:19,440
That was nothing. That was walking across
a carpet and touching a door handle.

94
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:24,200
This was different. This crackle was
deliberate.

95
00:06:25,120 --> 00:06:28,820
Another one from the fabric of her apron
toward the wall.

96
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:34,620
Another from the wool of her sleeve as she
reached to steady herself against the door

97
00:06:34,620 --> 00:06:39,700
frame. With every step she took across the
floor the sparks jumped.

98
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:45,580
The air was charged. Pressurized in a way that
made breathing feel like drawing something

99
00:06:45,580 --> 00:06:47,180
thicker than air into the lungs.

100
00:06:48,080 --> 00:06:52,680
Something that had weight, that resisted,
that wanted to remain where it was.

101
00:06:53,860 --> 00:06:55,280
The smell in the house.

102
00:06:56,380 --> 00:07:01,960
She knew the storm outside. She had walked
through it to get here and she knew

103
00:07:01,960 --> 00:07:06,300
what storms left behind. That sharp clear
smell.

104
00:07:07,580 --> 00:07:09,580
The smell of lightning working through
air.

105
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:16,440
Rearranging molecules. The smell that arrives
in the aftermath and tells you something

106
00:07:16,440 --> 00:07:21,480
electric has been through. She knew that
smell because she had lived on this plateau

107
00:07:21,480 --> 00:07:24,760
for 61 years and the plateau had storms.

108
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:28,180
The smell in the house was that smell.

109
00:07:29,480 --> 00:07:31,340
Except the storm was outside.

110
00:07:32,660 --> 00:07:36,260
She pushed the thought aside because there
was work to do.

111
00:07:37,120 --> 00:07:42,840
Duka Tesla. A woman with a memory like an
inventory and hands that had built her

112
00:07:42,840 --> 00:07:49,440
own tools. A woman who had memorized Serbian
epic poetry in lengths that would have exhausted

113
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:51,440
scholars was in labor.

114
00:07:52,500 --> 00:07:56,060
That was the work. The midwife moved
toward it.

115
00:07:56,760 --> 00:08:01,820
The contraction arrived with the next crack
of lightning outside and Duka's grip on the

116
00:08:01,820 --> 00:08:05,440
wooden rail of the headboard was not the
grip of a frightened woman.

117
00:08:06,340 --> 00:08:09,980
It was the grip of someone doing something
with all of themselves.

118
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:15,580
A grip that understood the thing being
done required everything available.

119
00:08:16,720 --> 00:08:18,740
The midwife's hands found their positions.

120
00:08:19,640 --> 00:08:23,140
The work was clear. Even when the room was
wrong.

121
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:29,220
Even when her skirts kept snapping with small
electric discharges she had no explanation

122
00:08:29,220 --> 00:08:35,780
for. Even when the air tasted of copper
and ozone and the specific metallic quality

123
00:08:35,780 --> 00:08:39,960
she associated with the sky just before
something struck.

124
00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:42,780
The storm outside built.

125
00:08:43,860 --> 00:08:50,100
Then it broke. At midnight, exactly, the
family would say later,

126
00:08:50,740 --> 00:08:55,420
passing this detail down through the chain
of oral tradition that became the documented

127
00:08:55,420 --> 00:08:58,080
account. The midwife said it first.

128
00:08:58,700 --> 00:09:00,400
The lightning is an omen.

129
00:09:01,400 --> 00:09:03,660
This child will be a child of darkness.

130
00:09:04,780 --> 00:09:10,020
This exchange enters the record through O
'Neill's biography and the biographical tradition,

131
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:12,840
not through Tesla's own memoir.

132
00:09:14,060 --> 00:09:16,400
Duka Tesla replied without hesitation.

133
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:20,260
No. He will be a child of light.

134
00:09:21,320 --> 00:09:24,500
At the stroke of midnight, the lightning
at its height,

135
00:09:25,340 --> 00:09:27,200
the thunder continuous overhead.

136
00:09:28,180 --> 00:09:30,740
A sound the valley would not forget.

137
00:09:31,940 --> 00:09:36,520
Nikola Tesla arrived. The midwife's hands
received him.

138
00:09:37,580 --> 00:09:42,860
This is the moment she would return to
later in the years that followed when she

139
00:09:42,860 --> 00:09:48,720
tried to account for the night, not the
sparks from her skirts or the metallic taste

140
00:09:48,720 --> 00:09:52,900
in the air. Or the way the hair had lifted
on her arms.

141
00:09:53,900 --> 00:09:58,800
The moment she would return to was the
weight of the arriving child in her hands.

142
00:09:59,780 --> 00:10:04,300
Not heavier than expected, not different
in any way she had language for.

143
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:08,700
But more present than the room around him.

144
00:10:09,880 --> 00:10:15,260
More there. As if the objects in the room
had their ordinary weight and presence,

145
00:10:16,080 --> 00:10:18,520
and this infant had a different quantity
of it.

146
00:10:19,320 --> 00:10:21,160
Had it at a different concentration.

147
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:24,760
The way a lightning rod concentrates
charge.

148
00:10:25,700 --> 00:10:29,440
She looked at his face. He was not crying.

149
00:10:31,020 --> 00:10:34,380
His eyes were open. The storm continued.

150
00:10:35,140 --> 00:10:38,360
The sparks continued. The smell in the
room.

151
00:10:39,160 --> 00:10:41,440
The outdoor smell. The storm smell.

152
00:10:42,300 --> 00:10:44,680
The smell of electrical discharge in open
air.

153
00:10:45,420 --> 00:10:47,440
Did not diminish when the birth was done.

154
00:10:48,340 --> 00:10:51,760
Did not clear the way a smell clears when
its source is removed.

155
00:10:52,940 --> 00:10:58,400
It remained. It occupied the room the way
a permanent quality of the air occupies a

156
00:10:58,400 --> 00:11:01,880
space. Not as residue, but as condition.

157
00:11:03,120 --> 00:11:05,960
The midwife would later describe the night
to her daughter,

158
00:11:06,780 --> 00:11:11,560
who would pass it to her daughter. Who
would eventually carry it to an old age

159
00:11:11,560 --> 00:11:14,860
in gossipage without ever quite knowing
what to do with it.

160
00:11:15,800 --> 00:11:19,680
Not the lightning. Lightning was ordinary
in the Lika region.

161
00:11:20,540 --> 00:11:24,020
Not the labor. She had attended scores of
them.

162
00:11:25,060 --> 00:11:26,800
The one thing she would return to.

163
00:11:27,740 --> 00:11:32,720
The one detail that resisted the comfortable
categories available to a practical woman

164
00:11:32,720 --> 00:11:37,140
who helped babies arrive in the world and
knew the business of it well.

165
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:42,420
The smell in the house was the smell of
lightning that had already struck.

166
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:48,000
And the lightning when she walked back out
through the storm was still in the sky.

167
00:11:49,060 --> 00:11:51,000
Something had arrived ahead of it.

168
00:11:52,120 --> 00:11:55,760
Below the Lika plateau, in the valley
town of Gospeach,

169
00:11:56,520 --> 00:12:00,240
15 kilometers down the limestone cast road
from Smeelyan,

170
00:12:00,860 --> 00:12:04,680
a school teacher named Stravko Blossich
was keeping a diary.

171
00:12:05,480 --> 00:12:07,480
Blossich was not a fanciful man.

172
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:10,460
The evidence for this is in the diary
itself,

173
00:12:11,280 --> 00:12:15,840
its texture, its patience, its attention
to the practical world,

174
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:21,580
weather, road conditions, the state of the
valley's livestock.

175
00:12:22,560 --> 00:12:27,360
The kind of information a man accumulates
when he has made a habit of observation and

176
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:32,440
has found over years of practice that the
observable world is more interesting than the

177
00:12:32,440 --> 00:12:37,620
interior one. He wrote an entry in July of
1856.

178
00:12:38,760 --> 00:12:45,380
The exact date water damaged in the
surviving page transcribed in a 1931 regional

179
00:12:45,380 --> 00:12:48,760
folklore pamphlet that exists in no
national archive.

180
00:12:49,680 --> 00:12:53,520
The entry notes. During the night of the
storm,

181
00:12:53,960 --> 00:12:56,080
the livestock in the valley did not
shelter.

182
00:12:57,140 --> 00:12:59,560
This is the thing Blossich found worth
recording.

183
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:02,220
Livestock shelter during storms.

184
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,860
They move toward enclosures, press against
fences,

185
00:13:06,460 --> 00:13:08,460
crowd into whatever protection is
available.

186
00:13:09,400 --> 00:13:13,160
This is what they do. It is what they have
always done.

187
00:13:14,460 --> 00:13:18,840
Blossich knew this because he had been
watching livestock in this valley for 30 years

188
00:13:18,840 --> 00:13:20,880
and they had always done it.

189
00:13:21,740 --> 00:13:26,740
Not this night. The livestock had moved
into the open.

190
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:30,500
They were standing in the open fields, all
of them,

191
00:13:31,040 --> 00:13:33,060
and they were facing the same direction.

192
00:13:33,800 --> 00:13:37,620
Not east, not toward town, not toward any
shelter.

193
00:13:38,760 --> 00:13:43,480
Blossich does not record the direction because
to him the direction was not the point.

194
00:13:44,360 --> 00:13:46,140
The orientation was the point.

195
00:13:46,980 --> 00:13:52,060
Every animal in those open fields facing
the same way as if they were being addressed

196
00:13:52,060 --> 00:13:57,720
by something that had yet to arrive or had already
arrived and they were simply acknowledging.

197
00:13:58,760 --> 00:14:02,720
He recorded this at dawn, walking out to
assess the valley.

198
00:14:03,660 --> 00:14:09,120
He wrote it with the sentence structure of
a man writing quickly before the mind's need

199
00:14:09,120 --> 00:14:12,220
for explanation could smooth the image
into comfort.

200
00:14:13,080 --> 00:14:17,060
He wrote it because the image had seated
itself in him and he could not move

201
00:14:17,060 --> 00:14:19,140
past it without committing it to paper.

202
00:14:20,500 --> 00:14:26,820
Then the well water. He drew from the well
the following morning and the water had

203
00:14:26,820 --> 00:14:31,380
a taste, not a bad taste, not a taste of
anything wrong.

204
00:14:32,420 --> 00:14:38,100
A taste where there usually was none, as if
the water had acquired something overnight

205
00:14:38,700 --> 00:14:42,900
or shed something that had been buffering
it and what remained was the water itself,

206
00:14:43,740 --> 00:14:46,960
unmediated, with its own character now
apparent.

207
00:14:48,140 --> 00:14:50,980
He describes it as sharper, more present.

208
00:14:52,300 --> 00:14:54,220
Then the light.

209
00:14:55,840 --> 00:14:59,560
The morning after the storm, the morning
of July 11th,

210
00:14:59,720 --> 00:15:06,060
1856, the light in the valley over Gospeach
looked in Blossich's words like an examination

211
00:15:06,060 --> 00:15:10,400
was being conducted. He wrote this and
underlined nothing.

212
00:15:11,640 --> 00:15:17,560
No exclamation. No note that this was unusual
language for a man who wrote about road

213
00:15:17,560 --> 00:15:23,640
conditions and livestock. He reached for
the nearest available description and wrote

214
00:15:23,640 --> 00:15:25,320
it and moved to the next line.

215
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:32,180
The light, like an examination, was being
conducted on the valley with a very white and

216
00:15:32,180 --> 00:15:36,800
particular quality, not the ordinary
summer light of the Lechoregion,

217
00:15:37,460 --> 00:15:40,580
which he knew as well as anyone who had
lived under it for decades,

218
00:15:41,920 --> 00:15:47,180
something with a tension in it, something that
was not simply illuminating but assessing.

219
00:15:48,140 --> 00:15:51,780
He knew nothing of the birth in the Tesla
household during the storm.

220
00:15:52,580 --> 00:15:54,920
He had no connection to the Tesla family.

221
00:15:55,980 --> 00:16:01,560
He was a schoolteacher in the valley below
and he was recording the observable world

222
00:16:01,560 --> 00:16:08,080
and the observable world on the morning of
July 11th had produced these observations,

223
00:16:09,020 --> 00:16:12,160
livestock in the open, facing the same
direction,

224
00:16:13,400 --> 00:16:17,500
water with a new taste, light like an
examination.

225
00:16:18,580 --> 00:16:23,020
He recorded them. He moved to the next
day's entry,

226
00:16:23,980 --> 00:16:26,960
road conditions. There is a second
account.

227
00:16:27,800 --> 00:16:31,400
It arrived not through paper but through
three generations of voice.

228
00:16:32,480 --> 00:16:38,780
In 1974, a graduate student from the University
of Zagreb traveled through the Lechoregion

229
00:16:38,780 --> 00:16:41,340
collecting fieldwork interviews on folk
memory.

230
00:16:42,060 --> 00:16:45,600
She recorded on cassette. She never
completed her thesis.

231
00:16:46,540 --> 00:16:51,020
The tape of the interview in question is
not in the digitized portion of the faculty

232
00:16:51,020 --> 00:16:53,480
archive if it was ever digitized.

233
00:16:54,160 --> 00:16:56,900
The woman she interviewed was named
Katarina.

234
00:16:57,860 --> 00:17:00,780
She was approximately 80 years old in
1974.

235
00:17:02,140 --> 00:17:07,280
Third generation memory. What she carried
was not observation but inheritance.

236
00:17:08,180 --> 00:17:12,740
The kind of memory that survives because
one person decided it was worth the act of

237
00:17:12,740 --> 00:17:16,280
passing on and then another person made
the same decision.

238
00:17:17,100 --> 00:17:22,000
Her grandmother had been a young woman
from Gospic in the summer of 1856.

239
00:17:23,020 --> 00:17:28,340
What the grandmother told her and what she
passed to the graduate student on a cassette

240
00:17:28,340 --> 00:17:32,020
whose location is now unknown was this.

241
00:17:33,020 --> 00:17:35,480
The morning after the birth night in
Smeleon,

242
00:17:36,160 --> 00:17:39,180
the sky looked clean in a way it hadn't
been before.

243
00:17:40,140 --> 00:17:42,260
Katarina repeated the distinction twice.

244
00:17:43,880 --> 00:17:45,900
Her grandmother had been particular about
it,

245
00:17:46,620 --> 00:17:50,120
not cleaner, not an improvement in weather
conditions,

246
00:17:50,780 --> 00:17:53,720
not the clearing of storm clouds to reveal
a better sky.

247
00:17:55,080 --> 00:18:01,740
A quality. The sky looked clean in a way
it hadn't been before as

248
00:18:01,740 --> 00:18:07,600
if something had been removed from it or
added and the result was a character the

249
00:18:07,600 --> 00:18:09,540
sky had not previously had.

250
00:18:11,260 --> 00:18:13,580
She was not a fanciful person.

251
00:18:14,280 --> 00:18:17,260
Katarina said of her grandmother in the
transcript.

252
00:18:18,340 --> 00:18:23,620
She was not the kind of woman who noticed
things but she noticed that.

253
00:18:24,780 --> 00:18:26,460
The grandmother said it once.

254
00:18:27,140 --> 00:18:28,760
Katarina told the graduate student.

255
00:18:29,380 --> 00:18:34,060
She said it and she saw that it had no
frame that there was nothing to

256
00:18:34,060 --> 00:18:36,340
attach it to and she didn't say it again.

257
00:18:37,440 --> 00:18:39,780
But she said it once.

258
00:18:40,640 --> 00:18:45,840
With the precision of someone recording an
observation they cannot account for but cannot

259
00:18:45,840 --> 00:18:50,180
let go of either. Katarina died before
1990.

260
00:18:51,080 --> 00:18:54,360
No family named. The transcript ends.

261
00:18:55,600 --> 00:18:58,840
And then there is the third source which
is institutional.

262
00:18:59,620 --> 00:19:05,160
The Austrian Imperial Meteorological Institute
maintained an observation station in Gospic

263
00:19:05,160 --> 00:19:07,620
as part of its network across the empire.

264
00:19:08,640 --> 00:19:11,740
Standard measurements, atmospheric
pressure,

265
00:19:11,740 --> 00:19:14,360
temperature, precipitation.

266
00:19:15,500 --> 00:19:20,740
The ordinary numbers of a system that
understood the sky as data to be collected and

267
00:19:20,740 --> 00:19:25,700
compared. The log entry for July 10th 1856
is standard.

268
00:19:26,580 --> 00:19:28,400
Summer conditions for the Lika region.

269
00:19:29,640 --> 00:19:31,440
Temperature within normal range.

270
00:19:32,240 --> 00:19:34,080
Precipitation significant overnight.

271
00:19:35,100 --> 00:19:36,580
Consistent with the storm.

272
00:19:37,700 --> 00:19:40,060
In the margin of the following day's
entry.

273
00:19:41,160 --> 00:19:42,820
July 11th.

274
00:19:42,820 --> 00:19:45,360
In a hand different from the standard
observers.

275
00:19:46,120 --> 00:19:49,240
Different pen. Different pressure on the
nib.

276
00:19:50,160 --> 00:19:53,200
Written by someone who did not sign their
notation.

277
00:19:54,260 --> 00:19:57,220
Atmospheric ionization reading anomalous
this morning.

278
00:19:58,140 --> 00:20:01,640
Instrument rechecked. No instrument fault
found.

279
00:20:02,440 --> 00:20:04,180
Will report if anomaly continues.

280
00:20:05,360 --> 00:20:07,100
There is no follow -up entry.

281
00:20:08,140 --> 00:20:12,860
So. A school teacher noting an examination
conducted in the light.

282
00:20:13,900 --> 00:20:17,360
A grandmother noting a sky that had
acquired a new character.

283
00:20:18,420 --> 00:20:21,760
An instrument detecting ionization it
could not account for.

284
00:20:22,320 --> 00:20:27,200
Recorded by a second observer who did not
sign the note and never followed up.

285
00:20:27,920 --> 00:20:32,840
Three instruments pointing at the same
morning from three different angles.

286
00:20:34,160 --> 00:20:36,280
None of them knew what the others had
seen.

287
00:20:37,680 --> 00:20:41,940
None of them knew that a child had been
born in the Tesla household during the

288
00:20:41,940 --> 00:20:46,120
storm. What arrived in the Smeleon Valley
that night.

289
00:20:46,880 --> 00:20:49,980
Arrived in the light. In the well water.

290
00:20:50,860 --> 00:20:52,680
In the atmospheric instruments.

291
00:20:53,560 --> 00:20:57,400
And in the bodies of every animal that
stood in the open fields.

292
00:20:58,280 --> 00:21:00,000
Facing the same direction.

293
00:21:00,940 --> 00:21:04,260
Until morning. The valley noticed.

294
00:21:05,180 --> 00:21:07,780
The standard biography of Nikola Tesla has
a shape.

295
00:21:08,440 --> 00:21:11,880
Born in a village in the Balkans. Arrives
in New York.

296
00:21:13,060 --> 00:21:15,140
Works for Edison. Then leaves.

297
00:21:16,260 --> 00:21:19,460
Creates alternating current. Wins the war
of currents.

298
00:21:20,320 --> 00:21:24,840
Lights the Niagara Falls. Sees his warden
cliff tower demolished for scrap.

299
00:21:25,480 --> 00:21:28,580
While his debts accumulate. Hotels.

300
00:21:29,380 --> 00:21:31,000
A sequence of hotels.

301
00:21:31,780 --> 00:21:33,180
Each one less than the last.

302
00:21:34,240 --> 00:21:36,060
As the investors fail to return.

303
00:21:37,100 --> 00:21:39,120
And the patents find no commercial home.

304
00:21:39,900 --> 00:21:42,300
And the mind that cannot stop producing
ideas.

305
00:21:42,740 --> 00:21:46,360
Continues producing them in rooms it
cannot adequately pay for.

306
00:21:47,280 --> 00:21:51,040
The shape is real. Every fact in it is
accurate.

307
00:21:51,940 --> 00:21:54,020
Every fact can be checked and found.

308
00:21:54,580 --> 00:21:59,020
And finding it. You feel the specific
gravity of a remarkable life.

309
00:21:59,400 --> 00:22:02,340
Lived at the intersection of genius and
loss.

310
00:22:03,320 --> 00:22:04,820
It is also insufficient.

311
00:22:05,800 --> 00:22:07,760
It describes the surface of a life.

312
00:22:08,060 --> 00:22:10,480
Without accounting for what produced that
surface.

313
00:22:11,200 --> 00:22:16,600
Nikola Tesla arrived at the port of New
York on June 6th 1884.

314
00:22:17,140 --> 00:22:18,780
With four cents in his pocket.

315
00:22:19,500 --> 00:22:22,160
And a letter of introduction to Thomas
Edison.

316
00:22:23,020 --> 00:22:27,000
He had been running machines in his mind
for years before that crossing.

317
00:22:27,960 --> 00:22:30,020
Not designs. Not sketches.

318
00:22:30,780 --> 00:22:33,560
Not rough models. Not approximations.

319
00:22:34,880 --> 00:22:37,600
Running machines. Complete.

320
00:22:38,240 --> 00:22:40,440
Functional. Mechanical systems.

321
00:22:41,120 --> 00:22:43,700
Operated entirely in a space behind his
eyes.

322
00:22:44,220 --> 00:22:49,000
With precision that his own account describes
in terms that require a moment to register.

323
00:22:49,520 --> 00:22:52,340
He wrote in his memoir My Inventions.

324
00:22:53,160 --> 00:22:56,100
Published in the electrical experimenter
in 1919.

325
00:22:57,120 --> 00:22:59,300
Exact words. Verifiable.

326
00:23:00,960 --> 00:23:06,140
It is absolutely immaterial to me whether
I run my turbine in thought or test it

327
00:23:06,140 --> 00:23:10,740
in my shop. Four sentences later in the
same text he wrote.

328
00:23:12,080 --> 00:23:15,440
Invariably my device works as I conceive
that it should.

329
00:23:15,980 --> 00:23:18,740
And the experiment comes out exactly as I
planned it.

330
00:23:19,560 --> 00:23:22,600
In 20 years there has not been a single
exception.

331
00:23:23,660 --> 00:23:26,480
Not rarely. Not almost always.

332
00:23:27,960 --> 00:23:31,360
20 years. Not a single exception.

333
00:23:32,500 --> 00:23:35,860
What kind of internal architecture
produces that accuracy?

334
00:23:36,640 --> 00:23:41,360
What kind of perception runs with zero error
across two decades of physical test and

335
00:23:41,360 --> 00:23:43,800
construction? Noting where on the
bearings.

336
00:23:44,320 --> 00:23:48,800
Correcting tolerances. Running the machines
until they reveal their own failure points.

337
00:23:49,520 --> 00:23:54,160
All without ever building a physical version
until the mental version is complete.

338
00:23:55,360 --> 00:23:57,160
The biographers call it visualization.

339
00:23:58,380 --> 00:24:01,860
Idetic memory. The gift of an
extraordinary mind.

340
00:24:02,940 --> 00:24:06,900
These are the right categories for a human
being of unusual capability.

341
00:24:07,980 --> 00:24:09,400
They are the categories available.

342
00:24:10,220 --> 00:24:16,000
But pull the childhood. Because the man
who arrived in New York with four cents was

343
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:21,580
already old by then. Old in the sense that
the structures of his perception had been

344
00:24:21,580 --> 00:24:23,960
in place since before he had language for
them.

345
00:24:25,060 --> 00:24:30,240
The boy who grew up in Smeleon had been
carrying something the biography's shape does

346
00:24:30,240 --> 00:24:32,880
not fully account for. Not a gift.

347
00:24:33,560 --> 00:24:36,660
A condition. He was three years old.

348
00:24:37,900 --> 00:24:41,040
Nighttime. The house in Smeleon.

349
00:24:42,080 --> 00:24:48,020
The cat's name in biographical tradition,
though not in Tesla's own autobiography,

350
00:24:48,680 --> 00:24:50,620
which gives only the animal and the event,

351
00:24:51,400 --> 00:24:54,800
was Makak. The Serbian word for Tomcat.

352
00:24:56,100 --> 00:25:00,620
A family cat. Large enough for a three
-year -old to press both hands flat against

353
00:25:00,620 --> 00:25:05,560
the back. Small enough for a three -year
-old to hold the position without effort.

354
00:25:06,600 --> 00:25:13,120
Nikola Tesla, age three, in a dark room,
stroking the cat's back in

355
00:25:13,120 --> 00:25:15,380
the darkness. The fuel.

356
00:25:16,460 --> 00:25:22,240
Warm. Not the warmth of the radiator or
the fire or any external source.

357
00:25:22,940 --> 00:25:26,580
The specific warmth of a living animal
generating its own heat.

358
00:25:27,600 --> 00:25:32,020
Slightly variable. The warmth of something
breathing beneath the surface.

359
00:25:33,440 --> 00:25:35,180
A three -year -old's hands are small.

360
00:25:36,700 --> 00:25:39,700
The fingers spread wide. Not gripping.

361
00:25:40,560 --> 00:25:43,200
Just resting on the back of an animal in
the dark.

362
00:25:44,240 --> 00:25:46,260
The grain of the fuel against the palms.

363
00:25:47,360 --> 00:25:49,360
Parting slightly under pressure.

364
00:25:50,600 --> 00:25:52,500
The body warmth rising through it.

365
00:25:53,580 --> 00:25:55,380
Then the fuel erupted.

366
00:25:56,360 --> 00:25:59,740
A sheet of sparks. Not one discharge.

367
00:26:00,680 --> 00:26:05,580
A continuous sheet of them fanning across
the entire back of the cat from the base

368
00:26:05,580 --> 00:26:07,380
of his tail toward his neck.

369
00:26:08,120 --> 00:26:10,580
Blue -white and absolute in the darkness.

370
00:26:11,800 --> 00:26:14,360
And for one instant the room was lit from
beneath.

371
00:26:15,020 --> 00:26:17,340
From the interface between fuel and palm.

372
00:26:17,820 --> 00:26:20,780
And the child's own hands were visible
from the inside.

373
00:26:22,180 --> 00:26:23,820
Backlit by the discharge.

374
00:26:24,720 --> 00:26:29,980
The small bones of his fingers silhouetted
in light that came from the animal under his

375
00:26:29,980 --> 00:26:32,560
hand. Then dark again.

376
00:26:33,780 --> 00:26:37,460
The sensation against his palms in the
instant before the light.

377
00:26:38,320 --> 00:26:41,140
Something between a pressure and a
release.

378
00:26:42,020 --> 00:26:45,700
A building that arrived and then completed
itself in the same instant.

379
00:26:46,260 --> 00:26:50,020
More like the moment before a sneeze than
anything in his prior experience.

380
00:26:50,020 --> 00:26:51,780
A gathering.

381
00:26:52,540 --> 00:26:54,800
A density. And then the discharge.

382
00:26:55,720 --> 00:27:00,040
And then the darkness. And the cat was
scrambling away.

383
00:27:01,120 --> 00:27:04,020
And the child's hands were empty and
tingling.

384
00:27:04,900 --> 00:27:08,640
And his eyes were full of the after -image
of his own fingers,

385
00:27:09,100 --> 00:27:15,180
lit from beneath. He asked his father,
what is this?

386
00:27:16,560 --> 00:27:23,140
His father, Milutin Tesla, was a Serbian
Orthodox priest who spoke several languages

387
00:27:23,140 --> 00:27:27,420
and could pass the Greek of the apostles
and the Latin of the church fathers.

388
00:27:28,320 --> 00:27:34,180
He was a man of logic and scripture and
the ordered universe that God had organized

389
00:27:34,180 --> 00:27:35,700
into comprehensible laws.

390
00:27:36,780 --> 00:27:38,540
He looked at his son's hands.

391
00:27:39,620 --> 00:27:44,500
He looked at the cat. He said,
electricity.

392
00:27:45,600 --> 00:27:50,940
The same thing as lightning. The boy had
been born during a lightning storm.

393
00:27:51,640 --> 00:27:53,820
The lightning had been in the air above
his birth.

394
00:27:54,540 --> 00:27:57,480
The smell of lightning had been in the
room of his birth.

395
00:27:58,360 --> 00:28:05,060
And now, at age three, in a dark room, he
had pulled lightning from an animal's

396
00:28:05,060 --> 00:28:09,480
back with his bare hands. And his father
had named what he pulled.

397
00:28:10,540 --> 00:28:12,260
He looked at his hands in the dark.

398
00:28:13,320 --> 00:28:17,140
Not frightened. This is the part that
requires a pause.

399
00:28:17,920 --> 00:28:20,880
Not frightened. More precise.

400
00:28:22,220 --> 00:28:26,760
The quality of a mind that has just received
a data point and is already organizing

401
00:28:26,760 --> 00:28:30,380
it. The biographers call this a formative
memory.

402
00:28:31,220 --> 00:28:35,100
The origin story of a man who would spend
his life working with electricity.

403
00:28:36,200 --> 00:28:38,720
That reading is available. It is accurate.

404
00:28:39,800 --> 00:28:43,720
But what kind of child, pulling fire from
a cat's back in the dark,

405
00:28:44,440 --> 00:28:47,440
responds not with fear, but with a
question.

406
00:28:48,440 --> 00:28:52,480
What kind of three -year -old reaches for
classification rather than flight?

407
00:28:53,440 --> 00:28:58,460
The mental engineering facility that would
not fail in 20 years of professional work

408
00:28:58,460 --> 00:29:00,860
was already in operation at age three.

409
00:29:01,580 --> 00:29:04,400
Already sorting. Already noting.

410
00:29:05,440 --> 00:29:09,140
From what? He was six years old when the
geometry arrived.

411
00:29:10,160 --> 00:29:12,780
Dinner. The Tesla household.

412
00:29:13,700 --> 00:29:15,900
The sounds of a family occupied with
eating.

413
00:29:16,500 --> 00:29:19,380
The ordinary sonic texture of a table in
use.

414
00:29:20,180 --> 00:29:25,820
Conversation. The particular busyness of family
time that fills a room and is not noticed

415
00:29:25,820 --> 00:29:29,180
until it is absent. Fork in his hand.

416
00:29:29,920 --> 00:29:33,180
Tin. Heavy for a child's hand.

417
00:29:34,040 --> 00:29:38,980
Heavier than it looks. Slightly warm from
being held for the minute it had been held.

418
00:29:39,980 --> 00:29:44,000
The seam where the handle was joined to
the tines detectable under his thumb.

419
00:29:44,680 --> 00:29:47,900
A small ridge that the thumb found and
returned to.

420
00:29:48,820 --> 00:29:50,480
Cold on the first touch.

421
00:29:51,620 --> 00:29:54,120
Body warm within 60 seconds of holding.

422
00:29:55,260 --> 00:29:58,100
The specific weight of an ordinary object.

423
00:29:59,320 --> 00:30:00,880
Then the fork was gone.

424
00:30:01,940 --> 00:30:04,160
He didn't fall. And he wasn't hiding it.

425
00:30:05,040 --> 00:30:06,540
Then his attention wandered.

426
00:30:07,420 --> 00:30:08,840
The fork was in his hand.

427
00:30:09,620 --> 00:30:11,200
And then his hand was holding nothing.

428
00:30:11,940 --> 00:30:15,300
And the table was gone. His mother's face
was gone.

429
00:30:16,180 --> 00:30:19,120
And the room was gone. And in their place.

430
00:30:19,860 --> 00:30:22,020
A flash of blinding light so complete.

431
00:30:22,540 --> 00:30:24,340
It erased the dining room entirely.

432
00:30:25,740 --> 00:30:27,760
Then. A place.

433
00:30:28,700 --> 00:30:31,760
Not the Tesla household. Not anywhere near
Smiljan.

434
00:30:32,760 --> 00:30:34,320
A scene he had been before.

435
00:30:35,020 --> 00:30:38,080
Or seen. Or carried somewhere in the back
of the mind.

436
00:30:38,500 --> 00:30:41,240
The way a child carries images they cannot
account for.

437
00:30:42,080 --> 00:30:43,760
The exact texture of a wall.

438
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:46,720
The quality of afternoon light against
stone.

439
00:30:47,560 --> 00:30:51,940
The particular geometry of a space he had
no business knowing at six years old.

440
00:30:52,980 --> 00:30:55,280
It was as real as the dinner table had
been.

441
00:30:56,420 --> 00:31:01,820
More real. In the way that something seen
in full daylight is more real than something

442
00:31:01,820 --> 00:31:05,660
glimpsed in shadow. The visual field had
not dimmed.

443
00:31:06,380 --> 00:31:13,040
It had been replaced. And what it was replaced
with was not dream logic or approximation.

444
00:31:14,320 --> 00:31:17,480
It was the high resolution particularity
of a place.

445
00:31:18,180 --> 00:31:20,560
Arriving with no explanation and no exit.

446
00:31:21,720 --> 00:31:26,760
He documented this in his memoir with the matter
of fact precision of a technical specification.

447
00:31:27,860 --> 00:31:29,840
The flashes of light. Blinding.

448
00:31:30,540 --> 00:31:34,140
Involuntary. Total. The images that
followed.

449
00:31:35,220 --> 00:31:37,440
Vivid scenes from places he had been.

450
00:31:37,820 --> 00:31:40,500
Or things he had seen. Arriving without
warning.

451
00:31:41,020 --> 00:31:43,560
So complete they displaced the actual
surroundings.

452
00:31:44,240 --> 00:31:46,720
He described learning to interrupt the
episodes.

453
00:31:47,300 --> 00:31:49,220
Developing techniques for shortening them.

454
00:31:49,900 --> 00:31:52,560
Finding ways to return to the ordinary
world faster.

455
00:31:53,600 --> 00:31:58,320
The language in the memoir is the language
of an engineer writing about a system he

456
00:31:58,320 --> 00:32:02,300
had to learn to manage. Here is a
phenomenon I experience.

457
00:32:03,300 --> 00:32:04,920
Here are the methods I applied.

458
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:08,040
The fork was still in his hand.

459
00:32:08,920 --> 00:32:11,440
He could feel it. The weight of it.

460
00:32:12,320 --> 00:32:16,520
The seam under his thumb. The tactile
world had not gone anywhere.

461
00:32:17,780 --> 00:32:20,000
Only the visual field had been replaced.

462
00:32:21,180 --> 00:32:24,920
And so he held a fork somewhere that was
not the dinner table.

463
00:32:25,580 --> 00:32:28,520
And the somewhere did not care that he was
holding a fork.

464
00:32:29,020 --> 00:32:32,280
And he could not turn it off any more than
he could turn off the sound

465
00:32:32,280 --> 00:32:35,700
of his own name. Then it receded.

466
00:32:36,720 --> 00:32:38,700
Not suddenly. Gradually.

467
00:32:39,640 --> 00:32:42,520
The way a heavy fog doesn't lift all at
once but thins.

468
00:32:43,120 --> 00:32:46,500
And the shapes behind it become visible as
shadows first.

469
00:32:47,660 --> 00:32:52,760
Then as outlines. Then as the full
resolution of the familiar world.

470
00:32:53,580 --> 00:32:57,220
The table. The smell of whatever was being
cooked.

471
00:32:57,940 --> 00:33:01,680
His mother's face. His mother was looking
at him.

472
00:33:03,120 --> 00:33:06,920
Her expression. She had seen his eyes go
somewhere.

473
00:33:08,120 --> 00:33:09,600
This was not the first time.

474
00:33:11,080 --> 00:33:13,140
The specific quality of her expression.

475
00:33:13,860 --> 00:33:16,220
Not alarmed. Not unalarmed.

476
00:33:17,380 --> 00:33:21,440
Something in the middle register of a woman
who has learned to monitor something in her

477
00:33:21,440 --> 00:33:24,160
child that she cannot explain and cannot
stop.

478
00:33:25,240 --> 00:33:26,700
Said she had been watching for it.

479
00:33:27,400 --> 00:33:30,020
Had perhaps been watching since before he
could remember.

480
00:33:31,120 --> 00:33:33,440
He could not tell her where he had gone.

481
00:33:34,180 --> 00:33:36,720
He did not have the words for where he had
gone.

482
00:33:37,420 --> 00:33:38,900
He had the fork in his hand.

483
00:33:39,580 --> 00:33:42,040
And the seam still under his thumb.

484
00:33:43,060 --> 00:33:44,580
And the smell of dinner.

485
00:33:45,900 --> 00:33:50,940
And his mother's face returning the specific
quality of attention of a woman who has

486
00:33:50,940 --> 00:33:52,920
already decided not to say anything.

487
00:33:53,700 --> 00:33:56,200
The visual episodes were not the only
phenomenon.

488
00:33:57,060 --> 00:34:02,300
There was a second separate behavior running
alongside them throughout Tesla's entire

489
00:34:02,300 --> 00:34:04,640
life. The need to count.

490
00:34:05,540 --> 00:34:09,360
The need to divide. Steps counted as he
walked them.

491
00:34:10,600 --> 00:34:12,420
Objects arranged in groups of three.

492
00:34:13,860 --> 00:34:16,460
The cubic volume of food calculated before
eating.

493
00:34:17,800 --> 00:34:19,800
Patterns built from multiples of three.

494
00:34:19,800 --> 00:34:21,300
In his movements.

495
00:34:22,060 --> 00:34:26,220
His habits. The number of times he circled
a building before entering.

496
00:34:27,500 --> 00:34:29,280
W. Bernard Carlson's biography.

497
00:34:30,440 --> 00:34:32,900
Tesla. Inventor of the electrical age.

498
00:34:33,680 --> 00:34:36,260
Princeton University Press. 2013.

499
00:34:37,280 --> 00:34:41,180
Documents this compulsion as consistent
from childhood through old age.

500
00:34:42,000 --> 00:34:44,160
Not a phase. Not a preference.

501
00:34:44,960 --> 00:34:49,200
A need. Its absence produced distress.

502
00:34:50,540 --> 00:34:53,740
Its satisfaction produced something the
record reads as relief.

503
00:34:55,340 --> 00:34:58,720
The biographers call it obsessive
compulsive disorder.

504
00:34:59,900 --> 00:35:03,000
The neurological texture of an
extraordinary mind.

505
00:35:03,900 --> 00:35:09,660
Maybe. That category exists for good reasons
and fits a great deal of what the record

506
00:35:09,660 --> 00:35:14,260
shows. But pull the two phenomena apart
and look at them separately.

507
00:35:15,260 --> 00:35:16,980
The flashes and vivid images.

508
00:35:18,100 --> 00:35:19,720
Something arriving from outside.

509
00:35:20,240 --> 00:35:22,240
Imposing itself on the visual field.

510
00:35:22,800 --> 00:35:25,800
Replacing the world with scenes he had not
chosen to see.

511
00:35:27,260 --> 00:35:28,820
The counting and dividing.

512
00:35:29,980 --> 00:35:32,780
A need to organize the world into
geometric units.

513
00:35:33,480 --> 00:35:35,680
Groups of three. Cubic measures.

514
00:35:35,680 --> 00:35:41,720
An internal compulsion moving outward to
impose structure on what was around him.

515
00:35:42,680 --> 00:35:44,760
What if they were not the same phenomenon?

516
00:35:45,760 --> 00:35:48,660
What if the counting was not the origin of
the geometry?

517
00:35:49,300 --> 00:35:55,100
But the response to it. The attempt to make
the visible world match something the visual

518
00:35:55,100 --> 00:35:56,760
episodes were already showing him.

519
00:35:57,580 --> 00:36:01,960
The six -year -old at the dinner table had
not looked for the replacement.

520
00:36:02,940 --> 00:36:07,500
It had found him. And it would not stop
finding him.

521
00:36:08,340 --> 00:36:10,000
The episodes returned.

522
00:36:10,900 --> 00:36:14,300
Uninvited. Departing on their own
cherdule.

523
00:36:14,780 --> 00:36:16,820
Without explanation or apology.

524
00:36:17,760 --> 00:36:19,400
He learned to manage them.

525
00:36:20,440 --> 00:36:23,180
He did not write about them as an
affliction.

526
00:36:24,120 --> 00:36:28,220
He wrote about them as data from a source
he had not identified.

527
00:36:29,400 --> 00:36:34,020
Donna Tesler died when Nicola was
approximately seven years old.

528
00:36:35,020 --> 00:36:39,920
The biographies place it variously between
1863 and 1865.

529
00:36:40,980 --> 00:36:43,660
The exact year disagreed upon across
sources.

530
00:36:44,840 --> 00:36:46,520
A horse accident in Smeleon.

531
00:36:47,480 --> 00:36:50,460
The details of the accident are thin in
the record.

532
00:36:51,200 --> 00:36:53,160
Tesla's own memoir gives only.

533
00:36:53,880 --> 00:36:55,280
I witnessed the tragic scene.

534
00:36:55,900 --> 00:37:01,120
That is all. A single sentence the
physical details absent.

535
00:37:02,400 --> 00:37:04,600
What the record preserves is different.

536
00:37:06,180 --> 00:37:10,560
The house. Nicola walking into the house
after Donna was gone.

537
00:37:11,280 --> 00:37:14,400
And the house being wrong in a way that
had no visible cause.

538
00:37:15,560 --> 00:37:18,160
Donna's coat still on the hook by the
door.

539
00:37:19,020 --> 00:37:20,420
Not Tesla's coat.

540
00:37:21,520 --> 00:37:28,080
Donna's coat. The specific ordinary object
of a coat on a hook which

541
00:37:28,080 --> 00:37:33,340
has always hung exactly like that coat on exactly
that hook and which now hangs differently.

542
00:37:34,200 --> 00:37:35,880
Not because the hook has changed.

543
00:37:36,500 --> 00:37:38,000
Not because the coat has changed.

544
00:37:39,100 --> 00:37:44,920
Because the coat hangs slightly differently
when the person who will take it down is

545
00:37:44,920 --> 00:37:46,540
no longer coming to take it down.

546
00:37:47,560 --> 00:37:50,420
The absence has gotten into the coat
somehow.

547
00:37:51,460 --> 00:37:54,160
Has affected its relationship with
gravity.

548
00:37:55,160 --> 00:37:58,600
The shoes beside the door. Worn leather.

549
00:38:00,060 --> 00:38:03,300
Broken in exactly the way one person's
foot breaks leather.

550
00:38:04,520 --> 00:38:06,940
The slight asymmetry of how Donna stood.

551
00:38:08,260 --> 00:38:10,880
The particular weight distribution of how
Donna walked.

552
00:38:12,240 --> 00:38:17,960
The specific shape of Donnie's heel and arch
and toe pressed into the leather over the

553
00:38:17,960 --> 00:38:21,980
years of wearing. Shoes learn their
wearer.

554
00:38:23,200 --> 00:38:27,900
These shoes had learned their wearer with
the patience of an object that spends years

555
00:38:27,900 --> 00:38:30,600
in close contact with a specific body.

556
00:38:31,620 --> 00:38:33,340
The body was not coming back.

557
00:38:34,740 --> 00:38:36,220
The shoes did not know this.

558
00:38:37,120 --> 00:38:39,460
They held the shape of someone who was not
there.

559
00:38:40,620 --> 00:38:42,860
Every room in the house was the wrong
size.

560
00:38:44,000 --> 00:38:48,880
Not smaller. This is not what happens to
rooms when a child has died in a

561
00:38:48,880 --> 00:38:51,140
family. They do not become smaller.

562
00:38:51,700 --> 00:38:53,500
They become wrongly proportioned.

563
00:38:53,920 --> 00:38:57,300
Too large. Not because anything physical
has changed.

564
00:38:58,220 --> 00:38:59,680
The walls are in the same positions.

565
00:39:00,480 --> 00:39:04,540
The ceiling is the same height. The
furniture has not been moved.

566
00:39:05,520 --> 00:39:08,840
But something that filled them is no
longer filling them.

567
00:39:09,620 --> 00:39:11,580
Something was occupying the rooms.

568
00:39:12,420 --> 00:39:14,820
Moving through them. Breathing in them.

569
00:39:15,520 --> 00:39:16,980
Having opinions about them.

570
00:39:17,420 --> 00:39:21,100
Making them occupied. And now that
occupying has stopped.

571
00:39:21,780 --> 00:39:26,260
And the emptiness that has replaced it is
not simply the absence of a person.

572
00:39:27,320 --> 00:39:29,020
It is the presence of an absence.

573
00:39:29,800 --> 00:39:32,280
Which is a different thing from ordinary
space.

574
00:39:33,240 --> 00:39:35,860
Nicola. Age seven or eight.

575
00:39:36,820 --> 00:39:39,280
The biographies disagree on the precise
year.

576
00:39:40,000 --> 00:39:43,720
Standing in a house where every room
announced its own wrong proportion.

577
00:39:44,820 --> 00:39:48,300
Standing in the space between Dona's coat
and Dona's shoes.

578
00:39:48,880 --> 00:39:51,180
And every room in the house being too
large.

579
00:39:51,580 --> 00:39:53,300
And having no language for any of it.

580
00:39:54,420 --> 00:39:57,400
After Don died. The visions intensified.

581
00:39:58,560 --> 00:40:02,300
This is documented. The flashes of
blinding light.

582
00:40:03,380 --> 00:40:06,240
The involuntary geometric replacement of
the visual field.

583
00:40:07,300 --> 00:40:09,700
These were not new after Dana's death.

584
00:40:10,860 --> 00:40:12,720
They had been present from early
childhood.

585
00:40:13,520 --> 00:40:15,100
Documented in Tesla's memoir.

586
00:40:15,660 --> 00:40:19,140
As a condition of his perception from
before his memory began.

587
00:40:20,000 --> 00:40:22,320
What changed after Dona's was their
frequency.

588
00:40:23,560 --> 00:40:28,160
Their vividness. The aperture through
which they arrived seemed to have widened.

589
00:40:28,820 --> 00:40:30,340
And through that wider aperture.

590
00:40:31,080 --> 00:40:33,620
The geometry came more often and stayed
longer.

591
00:40:34,780 --> 00:40:36,820
The biographers attribute this to grief.

592
00:40:37,780 --> 00:40:41,120
The worsening visions a sensitive child's
response to loss.

593
00:40:42,240 --> 00:40:45,320
The nervous system temporarily
dysregulated by trauma.

594
00:40:45,760 --> 00:40:48,120
Producing intensified hallucinatory
episodes.

595
00:40:49,440 --> 00:40:52,860
That reading is not wrong. Grief does
things to perception.

596
00:40:53,860 --> 00:40:55,280
The record confirms this.

597
00:40:56,060 --> 00:40:58,100
But the visions were not produced by
grief.

598
00:40:58,980 --> 00:41:03,020
They preceded grief. They were there
before Dona was gone.

599
00:41:04,100 --> 00:41:09,680
What grief did. If this is grief's work is
not create the phenomenon.

600
00:41:10,240 --> 00:41:13,440
But open a larger passage through which it
could arrive.

601
00:41:14,260 --> 00:41:19,600
And this requires a different question from
what happened to this grieving child's nervous

602
00:41:19,600 --> 00:41:22,820
system. The grief is not in doubt.

603
00:41:23,560 --> 00:41:27,280
The grief was real and lasting and marked
him.

604
00:41:28,140 --> 00:41:33,740
Tesla wrote about Dona in his memoir with the
specific weight of someone who has carried

605
00:41:33,740 --> 00:41:38,440
a loss for decades. And has not resolved
it because it is not the kind of

606
00:41:38,440 --> 00:41:41,940
loss that resolves. He was the celebrated
child.

607
00:41:42,780 --> 00:41:48,540
The exceptional one. The one toward whom the
household's pride moved naturally without

608
00:41:48,540 --> 00:41:52,260
effort. He was the one the family was
organized around.

609
00:41:53,000 --> 00:41:54,400
And then he was not.

610
00:41:55,560 --> 00:41:59,800
What changed in Nicola after Dona was gone
is not that his visions began.

611
00:42:00,660 --> 00:42:06,400
They were already there. What changed is
that the house had the wrong proportions and

612
00:42:06,400 --> 00:42:10,680
the coat was on the hook and the shoes
held a foot that would not return

613
00:42:10,680 --> 00:42:17,200
to them. And in that wrongly proportioned
house with those shoes with that coat.

614
00:42:18,160 --> 00:42:24,320
The geometry came more often as if whatever
was already present in his perception had

615
00:42:24,320 --> 00:42:29,940
found in the specific openness of a child
in the middle of the first great loss

616
00:42:30,640 --> 00:42:33,480
A wider door. Now.

617
00:42:34,700 --> 00:42:37,660
The shape of the life. The immigrant
crossing.

618
00:42:38,240 --> 00:42:40,380
The Edison rivalry. The war of currents.

619
00:42:41,540 --> 00:42:43,780
All of this is known. Documented.

620
00:42:44,420 --> 00:42:46,780
Verifiable. The short version.

621
00:42:47,540 --> 00:42:52,840
Tesla won. His alternating current system
became the dominant standard for electrical

622
00:42:52,840 --> 00:42:58,160
power distribution worldwide. The long
version is the shape of how he won and what it

623
00:42:58,160 --> 00:43:04,500
cost. He gave George Westinghouse his Niagara
patents for far less than their value because

624
00:43:04,500 --> 00:43:07,780
Westinghouse told him the company could
not survive otherwise.

625
00:43:08,720 --> 00:43:14,440
The traditional account says Tesla voluntarily
tore up the royalty contract that he handed

626
00:43:14,440 --> 00:43:19,400
over the basis of the electrical century
as an act of will not of necessity.

627
00:43:20,620 --> 00:43:22,940
Modern historians debate that framing.

628
00:43:24,160 --> 00:43:30,180
What is not in dispute? Tesla received far
less than those patents were worth and the

629
00:43:30,180 --> 00:43:32,420
century they powered did not reciprocate.

630
00:43:33,200 --> 00:43:39,260
Then Wardenclyffe, a tower on Long Island
designed to transmit electrical power wirelessly

631
00:43:39,260 --> 00:43:42,080
across the planet. Not through wires.

632
00:43:42,800 --> 00:43:46,780
Through the earth itself. The planet was a
conductor,

633
00:43:47,440 --> 00:43:51,680
which it is. And if you could induce the
right resonant frequency in it,

634
00:43:52,260 --> 00:43:57,620
you could make energy as ubiquitous as air
present everywhere available to anyone.

635
00:43:58,480 --> 00:44:01,080
His primary investor was JP Morgan.

636
00:44:02,140 --> 00:44:06,700
When Morgan understood that Tesla had
expanded the project far beyond their original

637
00:44:06,700 --> 00:44:11,980
agreement from a wireless communication
station to something vastly more ambitious,

638
00:44:12,660 --> 00:44:15,300
he refused to provide additional funds.

639
00:44:16,140 --> 00:44:17,620
The tower stopped.

640
00:44:19,080 --> 00:44:22,700
Wardenclyffe was demolished for scrap
during the First World War.

641
00:44:23,640 --> 00:44:26,660
The scrap did not cover what Tesla owed.

642
00:44:27,820 --> 00:44:34,020
From there, hotels, a sequence of them
moving as the debts became impossible.

643
00:44:35,080 --> 00:44:39,220
The Hotel New Yorker, with assistance from
Westinghouse Electric Company,

644
00:44:39,860 --> 00:44:46,360
arranged him a room, part nominal rate, part
the prestige of having Nikola Tesla as a

645
00:44:46,360 --> 00:44:48,140
resident on the 33rd floor.

646
00:44:49,060 --> 00:44:52,180
He accepted. He had no other option.

647
00:44:53,320 --> 00:44:59,360
He was 77 years old, and the century he
had powered was not paying him back.

648
00:45:00,260 --> 00:45:05,660
And in that room, in the room that had
been assigned to him in exchange for

649
00:45:05,660 --> 00:45:09,020
his name, he could not find his kind.

650
00:45:10,320 --> 00:45:12,400
This is not a biographical observation.

651
00:45:13,240 --> 00:45:18,600
This is documented in the way that things
a person cannot say directly are sometimes

652
00:45:18,600 --> 00:45:20,620
documented in what they do say.

653
00:45:21,460 --> 00:45:24,160
Tesla wrote about people with genuine
interest,

654
00:45:24,680 --> 00:45:28,540
occasionally with warmth. But the warmth
had a quality.

655
00:45:29,460 --> 00:45:33,020
The warmth of someone watching a species
they found fascinating,

656
00:45:33,020 --> 00:45:35,600
rather than sharing territory with them.

657
00:45:36,420 --> 00:45:38,360
He was fluent in eight languages.

658
00:45:39,260 --> 00:45:43,700
He could hold a conversation that left the
other party electrified and breathless.

659
00:45:44,620 --> 00:45:49,500
He was, when he chose to be, a social
creature of considerable skill.

660
00:45:50,760 --> 00:45:54,780
But underneath the skill, a distance that
did not close.

661
00:45:55,940 --> 00:46:01,500
He spent 86 years looking in the coded way
that a being looks for its kind

662
00:46:01,500 --> 00:46:05,920
when it has no certainty that its kind
exists on this side of anything.

663
00:46:06,760 --> 00:46:09,680
Human eyes looked at him and found an
inventor,

664
00:46:10,520 --> 00:46:17,380
a genius, an eccentric, a ruin, the
various versions of the story available.

665
00:46:18,320 --> 00:46:22,520
He looked back through those eyes and
found no recognition.

666
00:46:23,440 --> 00:46:26,460
He left the window of his room open in
January,

667
00:46:27,220 --> 00:46:30,240
not by accident, not because he had
forgotten,

668
00:46:31,200 --> 00:46:33,000
not because the room had overheated.

669
00:46:34,080 --> 00:46:36,680
He left it open because she might come.

670
00:46:38,080 --> 00:46:44,840
The window open and the cold of January
Manhattan pouring in through the gap and

671
00:46:44,840 --> 00:46:48,860
the napkins on the desk stirring slightly
in the draft as he wrote.

672
00:46:48,860 --> 00:46:55,080
And the equations crossing the borders of
the napkins without registering the edges.

673
00:46:56,920 --> 00:46:59,320
Work. He was still doing work.

674
00:47:00,180 --> 00:47:03,360
The work was not finished and it would not
be finished.

675
00:47:04,040 --> 00:47:09,220
And he was still working toward it with
the methodical precision of a man who had

676
00:47:09,220 --> 00:47:14,920
run machines without error for twenty years
and had never stopped expecting himself to

677
00:47:14,920 --> 00:47:17,480
do the same. The napkins.

678
00:47:18,440 --> 00:47:23,300
Hotel napkins. The only surface available
in quantity.

679
00:47:24,080 --> 00:47:26,740
The equations that covered them were real
mathematics.

680
00:47:27,720 --> 00:47:33,300
Active thought. Genuine science conducted in a
space too small for the scale of the thinking.

681
00:47:34,140 --> 00:47:38,280
The way a map of a continent drawn on a
grain of rice is still a

682
00:47:38,280 --> 00:47:43,680
map of a continent. And the window open
for her.

683
00:47:45,340 --> 00:47:49,400
White. O'Neill's biography records Tesla's
description.

684
00:47:50,460 --> 00:47:54,340
A beautiful bird. Pure white with light
gray tips on its wings.

685
00:47:55,320 --> 00:47:57,600
She had come to him in the years before
this one.

686
00:47:58,220 --> 00:47:59,680
Had found his window and returned.

687
00:48:00,920 --> 00:48:04,640
Had made a habit of his window in the way
that animals make habits of the

688
00:48:04,640 --> 00:48:07,960
places where they are known. He had fed
her daily.

689
00:48:09,120 --> 00:48:11,160
He had arranged his schedule around her
visit.

690
00:48:12,160 --> 00:48:14,980
When she was injured he had brought her
inside.

691
00:48:16,500 --> 00:48:20,320
She had looked at him. This is the part
that requires the pause.

692
00:48:21,580 --> 00:48:25,400
Not. She was a pretty bird and he was a
lonely old man.

693
00:48:26,340 --> 00:48:29,380
And the attachment is understandable and a
little sad.

694
00:48:29,380 --> 00:48:31,700
That reading is available.

695
00:48:32,700 --> 00:48:34,600
It misses the specific word he used.

696
00:48:35,320 --> 00:48:37,180
Tesla, who was not imprecise.

697
00:48:37,900 --> 00:48:41,540
Who had spent his life working toward
precision in everything he put words to.

698
00:48:42,080 --> 00:48:45,120
Describe the experience of the pigeons
gaze as recognition.

699
00:48:46,020 --> 00:48:49,080
Not affection. Not interest.

700
00:48:50,580 --> 00:48:55,260
Recognition. Human eyes had looked at him
for 86 years.

701
00:48:56,080 --> 00:48:58,240
He had been studied. Celebrated.

702
00:48:59,140 --> 00:49:00,660
Dismissed. Exploited.

703
00:49:01,640 --> 00:49:04,100
Admired. And declared harmless.

704
00:49:05,060 --> 00:49:08,780
Not once in all of that looking had he
found what he found when this bird

705
00:49:08,780 --> 00:49:12,360
looked at him. O'Neill records him saying
